.TH MAILBBS 1 "12 October 2026" Linux "FPAC Operator's Manual"
.SH NAME 
mailbbs \- leaves a personal message on a BBS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mailbbs \-d callsign[@bbs] [\-f filename] [\-i sender] [\-t title] [\-v] port bbs-callsign [[via] digis...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
.B mailbbs
connects to a BBS in AX.25, NET/ROM or ROSE, according to the kind of
.I port,
leaves one personal message (SP) and disconnects. The text is taken from a
file or from the standard input. It can be used from a script to send
statistics, an alert or a change of status of the node to its sysop.
.P
It is not a daemon and no FPAC program needs it. Since 4.1.6-rc8
.B myfpac.sh
no longer runs it at each start of the node.
.P
For a ROSE port the arguments are: port, callsign of the BBS, ROSE address,
then optionally "via" and one digipeater.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 13
.BI "\-d " callsign[@bbs]
Recipient of the message. Needed.
.TP 13
.BI "\-f " filename
File holding the text. Without \-f the standard input is sent, unless it is a
terminal: the message then has a title and no text.
.TP 13
.BI "\-i " sender
Callsign the connection is made from. Default: the callsign of the port.
.TP 13
.BI "\-t " title
Subject of the message. Default: "message fm mailbbs".
.TP 13
.BI \-v
Show the dialogue with the BBS.
.TP 13
.BI \-V
Show the version and exit.
.TP 13
.BI "\-h, \-?"
Show a short help and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 5
0
the BBS took the message.
.TP 5
1
bad arguments, unknown port or file that cannot be read.
.TP 5
2
the BBS could not be connected (120 seconds at most).
.TP 5
3
no answer for 60 seconds, dialogue longer than 5 minutes, unexpected
disconnection or message refused by the BBS. The reason is written on the
standard error output.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
date | mailbbs \-d SYSOP \-t "Node restarted" rose0 F6BVP-1 2080175520
mailbbs \-d F6BVP@F6BVP \-t "Statistics" \-f /usr/local/var/ax25/fpac/fpacstat.day axudp F6BVP-1
.EE
.SH NOTES
mailbbs presents itself to the BBS with a banner, [MAIL-version-$], as a
forwarding program does. The BBS must accept such a connection from the
callsign used.
